\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chi-Square Distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Chi-Square 
\begin_inset Formula $(χ^{2})$
\end_inset

 distribution is the best method to test a population variance against a known or assumed value of the population.
 A Chi-Square distribution is a continuous distribution with degrees of freedom.
 As the degrees of freedom increase,
 the chi-square curve becomes more symmetric and approaches a normal distribution.
 Six Sigma practitioners (Green Belts and Black Belts) encounter the chi-square distribution when they need to analyze variability or test hypotheses about categorical data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The best part of a Chi-Square distribution is that it describes the distribution of a sum of squared random variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is also used to test the goodness of fit of data distribution,
 whether or not a data series is independent and for judging the confidences surrounding variance and standard deviation for a random variable from a normal distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This guide explains what the chi-square distribution is,
 why it’s important in Six Sigma,
 how to conduct various chi-square hypothesis tests and when to use each test in a process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
What Is the Chi-Square Distribution?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The chi-square distribution is a family of distributions defined by a parameter called degrees of freedom (df).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Intuitively you can think of degrees of freedom as being related to sample size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example if you sum the squares of k independent standard normal variables the result follows a 
\begin_inset Formula $χ^{2}$
\end_inset

 distribution with k degrees of freedom.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Key properties of the chi-square distribution include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Non-negativity:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $χ^{2}$
\end_inset

 ² values range from 0 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset

 (no negative values as it’s a sum of squares).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Shape depends on degrees of freedom:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 With low df the distribution is highly skewed right (a long tail to the right).
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

As df increases the distribution becomes more bell-shaped and approaches symmetry.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Mean and variance:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The mean of a 
\begin_inset Formula $χ^{2}$
\end_inset

 distribution is equal to its degrees of freedom and the variance is twice the degrees of freedom.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

For example,
 a 
\begin_inset Formula $χ^{2}$
\end_inset

 ² with df = 6 has mean 6 and variance 12.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Relation to normal distribution:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 A chi-square distribution with a large df (say df ≥ 30) is approximately normal.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 In fact as df increases the 
\begin_inset Formula $χ^{2}$
\end_inset

 probability density function begins to look symmetric like the normal curve.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These properties mean that small 
\begin_inset Formula $χ^{2}$
\end_inset

 values cluster near zero and large values are rarer (in the right tail).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This characteristic is what makes the chi-square distribution useful for hypothesis testing.
 We use it to quantify how far our observed data deviate from expectation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In practice chi-square statistics often measure the discrepancy between observed data and what we expect under some hypothesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the chi-square statistic is small it suggests that the observed data are close to what was expected.
 If it’s large it means there’s a big gap between observation and expectation so large 
\begin_inset Formula $χ^{2}$
\end_inset

 values often indicate that the observed pattern is unlikely to be due to random chance under the null hypothesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Why Is Chi-Square Important in Six Sigma?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Six Sigma projects focus on reducing defects and variability.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While many Six Sigma analyses involve means and the normal distribution practitioners also need to understand variability and categorical data relationships.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The chi-square distribution is central to several hypothesis tests that Six Sigma teams use to analyze variation and categorical factors in the Analyze phase (and sometimes in Measure/Improve phases).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Usually the goal of the Six Sigma team is to find the level of variation of the output,
 not just the mean.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Chi-square tests help in this pursuit by enabling you to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Evaluate process variance:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 The chi-square is the distribution used to test a population variance or standard deviation against a target or baseline.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

For example,
 you might verify in the Measure phase that a process’s variance is within an acceptable range or in the Improve phase that an improvement initiative significantly reduced variability.
 (This is done via the chi-square test for a single variance described later.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Assess distribution fit:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Six Sigma often assumes normality for process metrics but what if the data are not normal?
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

A chi-square goodness-of-fit test can check if your data follow a particular distribution (e.g.,
 normal,
 Poisson,
 etc.).
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 This could occur in Measure/Analyze when you need to validate distribution assumptions for capability analysis or determine if a count of defects fits a Poisson model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Test for independence (association):
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 In Analyze you may need to determine whether two categorical factors are related.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 For example,
 are defect types independent of the machine or shift?
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

A chi-square test of independence can reveal whether there is a statistically significant association between two categorical variables (e.g.,
 factor and defect occurrence) which can help to pinpoint root causes.
 If the test shows dependence you’ve found a clue on where to focus improvements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Analyze frequency counts and proportions:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Chi-square tests handle data in frequency form (counts in categories).
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

This is common in survey data or whenever outcomes are classified into categories.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

For instance a Six Sigma team might survey customers about preferences (categorical responses) or classify defect reasons into categories:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 chi-square analysis can determine if those frequencies differ from expectations or differ across groups.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In summary the chi-square distribution underpins tools for variation analysis and categorical data analysis in Six Sigma.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is especially relevant during Analyze (hypothesis testing for root cause analysis) but also appears in Measure (for baseline distribution checks or variance tests) and Improve (to confirm changes in variation).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Knowing when and how to use chi-square tests during DMAIC ensures you select the right tool for questions about variability or categorical factors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
History of Chi-Square
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Karl Pearson (1857 to 1936) founded the first statistics department in the world at University College London) and came up with the Chi-Square distribution.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Pearson’s work in statistics began when he developed a mathematical method for studying the process of heredity and evolution.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Later the Chi-Square distribution came about as Pearson tried to find a measure of the goodness of fit of other distributions to random variables in his heredity model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Chi-Square Statistic
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A Chi-Square distribution may skew to the right or with a long tail toward the significant values of the distribution.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The overall shape of the distribution will depend on the number of degrees of freedom in a given problem.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The degree of freedom is one less than the sample size.
\end_layout

