\begin_layout Section
Chi-Square Properties
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The mean of the distribution is equal to the number of degrees of freedom:
 
\begin_inset Formula $μ=ϑ$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The variance equals two times the number of degrees of freedom:
 
\begin_inset Formula $σ^{2}=2*ϑ$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the degrees of freedom are greater than or equal to 2,
 the maximum value for Y occurs when 
\begin_inset Formula $χ^{2}=ϑ-2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the degrees of freedom increase the Chi-Square curve approaches a normal distribution and the symmetry of the graph also increases.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally the distribution may be skewed to the right and as the random variable it is based on is squared it has no negative values.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the degrees of freedom increase the probability density function begins to appear symmetrical.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The formula for the probability density function of the Chi-Square distribution is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
f\left(x\right) & = & \frac{e^{-\frac{x}{2}}\frac{\vartheta}{x^{2}}-1}{2^{\frac{\vartheta}{2}}\Gamma\left(\frac{\delta}{2}\right)}\,\,\,for\,x\geq0
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where 
\begin_inset Formula $ϑ$
\end_inset

 is the shape parameter and 
\begin_inset Formula $Γ$
\end_inset

 is the gamma function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The formula for the gamma function is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\varGamma\left(a\right) & = & \int^{\infty}_{0}e^{a-1}e^{-t}dt
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Chi-Square Hypothesis Test
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Usually the goal of the Six Sigma team is to find the level of variation of the output,
 not just the mean of the population.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Above all the team would like to know how much variation the production process shows about the target to see what changes are needed to reach a process free of defects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a comparison between several sample variances or a comparison between frequency proportions the standard test statistic called the Chi-Square 
\begin_inset Formula $χ^{2}$
\end_inset

 test will be used.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So,
 the distribution of the Chi-Square statistic is called the Chi-Square distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Types of Chi-Square Hypothesis Tests
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Chi-Square test is best suited for:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Case
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

I:
 Testing whether a sample’s variance matches a known population variance (Chi-Square test for a single variance).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Case
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

II:
 Testing whether observed frequencies differ significantly from expected frequencies,
 as in tests of independence or goodness-of-fit with categorical data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I’ll give an overview of each below and link to pages where you can find more information on exactly how to perform both.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A hypothesis test is used to assess claims about a population parameter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Whether or not the test is one-tailed or two-tailed depends on the direction of the alternative hypothesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A one-tailed test checks whether a parameter is significantly greater than or less than a certain value (rejection area in one tail).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A two-tailed test checks whether a parameter is different from the hypothesized value (rejection areas in both tails).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Chi-square test is commonly used for:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Goodness-of-fit tests.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Tests of independence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Tests for a single population variance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Key characteristics of the Chi-square distribution:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
It is non-negative (values ≥ 0).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It is not symmetric it is right-skewed.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The shape depends on the degrees of freedom (df)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For a variance test:
 
\begin_inset Formula $df=n−1df=n–1df=n−1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Important distinction:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When testing frequency data (e.g.,
 for independence or goodness-of-fit) you do not need the population variance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When testing frequency data (e.g.,
 for independence or goodness-of-fit),
 you do not need the population variance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When testing a single variance you must know the population variance to form your null hypothesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Right-Tailed vs.
 Left-Tailed vs.
 Two-Tailed Chi-Square Tests
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Most chi-square hypothesis tests are naturally one-tailed but it’s important to understand which tail (right or left) is being used.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The chi-square distribution is not symmetric and is typically right-skewed so the “tail” refers to the upper end (right tail) or lower end (left tail) of the distribution curve.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Left-tailed vs.
 right-tailed regions in a χ² distribution (SixSigmaStudyGuide.com).
 In a right-tailed test,
 large χ² values (far right) indicate significance.
 In a left-tailed test,
 very small χ² values (far left) indicate significance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Right-Tailed Tests:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 By far the most common case.
 Goodness-of-fit and independence tests are almost always right-tailed.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Why?
 Because the test statistic in these cases is 
\begin_inset Formula $χ²=Σ(O−E)²/E$
\end_inset

 which is always positive.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 The greater the discrepancy between Observed (O) and Expected (E) frequencies the larger 
\begin_inset Formula $χ²$
\end_inset

 becomes pushing it into the right tail of the distribution.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 If the 
\begin_inset Formula $χ²$
\end_inset

 value is extremely large (beyond a critical value in the right tail) it indicates that the observed frequencies deviate significantly from expectations and we reject the null hypothesis.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 In other words,
 these tests only have one rejection region on the high side of the χ² curve.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Left-Tailed Tests:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

These are rare for chi-square but do occur in specific scenarios,
 particularly the chi-square test for a single variance when you want to see if a variance is significantly smaller than a target value.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

In a left-tailed 
\begin_inset Formula $χ²$
\end_inset

 test the rejection region is in the left tail (low values) of the distribution.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 A small 
\begin_inset Formula $χ²$
\end_inset

 statistic indicates observed variance is much lower than expected.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

For example,
 if you hypothesize that a new process’s variance is less than the old variance you’d use a left-tailed test (lower variance yields a smaller 
\begin_inset Formula $χ²$
\end_inset

 value).
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 If the test statistic falls below a critical value near the left tail then reject the null and conclude the variance is significantly lower.
 (We’ll see an example with queue wait times shortly.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Two-Tailed Tests:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 There is no built-in two-tailed version of the chi-square in the way we have for Z or t (because χ² ≥ 0 only).
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 However,
 you can conduct a two-tailed test on variance by splitting α into both tails of the 
\begin_inset Formula $χ²$
\end_inset

 distribution.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

This is used if you want to detect any significant difference (either increase or decrease) in variance.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 In practice,
 a two-tailed chi-square test for variance means you have two critical values:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 one in the right tail and one in the left tail.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

You would reject the null hypothesis if the 
\begin_inset Formula $χ²$
\end_inset

 statistic is either too high (above the upper critical value) or too low (below the lower critical value).
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Two-tailed chi-square tests are less common in Six Sigma because usually one is checking either for an increase or a decrease in variation not just any change.
 But it’s conceptually similar to two-tailed tests elsewhere.
 You’re defining an extreme region on both ends of the distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Selecting the Correct Tail:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A common mistake is confusing which tail to use.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Remember that for goodness-of-fit and independence tests you always use the right tail:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
a large 
\begin_inset Formula $χ²$
\end_inset

 is the signal that the observed data don’t match the null hypothesis expectation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For variance tests it depends on your alternative hypothesis:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $H₁:σ²>σ₀²$
\end_inset

 (looking for greater variability):
 use a right-tailed test.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $H₁:σ²<σ₀²$
\end_inset

 (looking for reduced variability):
 use a left-tailed test.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $H₁:σ²≠σ₀²$
\end_inset

 (any difference):
 use a two-tailed approach (both tails).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the sections below we’ll highlight the tail for each type of test.
 We’ll also discuss how to find the correct critical values from a chi-square table for each case in a later section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Chi-Square Goodness-of-Fit Test
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A chi-square goodness-of-fit (GOF) test checks how well an observed categorical data distribution fits an expected distribution.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In other words it tests whether or not the frequencies observed in different categories match some hypothesized frequencies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is useful when you have one categorical variable and a theory or historical data about what the distribution of that variable should be.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example,
 you might want to verify if defect occurrences follow a Poisson distribution or if customer preferences are evenly distributed among choices.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The chi-square GOF test is a non-parametric test (no assumption of normality;
 it deals directly with category counts) (see the chi-square goodness of fit test).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
When to use:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Use a GOF test in Six Sigma during Measure or Analyze when you need to validate distributional assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For instance,
 if you assume defects per hour follow a Poisson distribution then a GOF test can confirm or refute that.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Or if historical data suggest a certain proportional breakdown of defect causes you can test if current data still fits that pattern.
 This helps ensure that you’re using the correct statistical models and can reveal changes in process behavior.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Hypotheses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Typically,
 for a goodness-of-fit test:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $H₀$
\end_inset

 (null hypothesis):
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The observed distribution fits the expected distribution.
 (No significant difference between observed and expected frequencies.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $H₁$
\end_inset

 (alternative hypothesis):
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The observed distribution does not fit the expected distribution (at least one category’s frequency is significantly different from expected).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In plain terms,
 
\begin_inset Formula $H₀$
\end_inset

 assumes that any differences between observed and expected counts are just due to random chance,
 while 
\begin_inset Formula $H₁$
\end_inset

 asserts that the differences are too large to be random thereby indicating that the distribution has changed or the assumption was wrong.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each observation falls into one of the categories and you have a sufficiently large sample such that expected counts in each category aren’t too low.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A common rule of thumb is that expected counts should be at least 5 in each category (or if that’s not possible,
 no more than 20% of categories have expected < 5) (see the chi-square goodness of fit test).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If expected frequencies are very small,
 the chi-square approximation may not be accurate (you might need to combine categories or use an exact test).
\end_layout

